theory.intro:
To draw an angle of a given size, use a protractor to mark the second arm at the correct degree.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes,calc}
\draw (2.6,0) arc (0:180:2.6);
\draw (2.2,0) arc (0:180:2.2);
\draw[gray] (-2.6,0)--(0,0);
\foreach \a in {0,10,...,180}{\draw (\a:2.2)--(\a:2.6);}
\coordinate (V) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (3.4,0);
\coordinate (B) at (62:3.4);
\draw[very thick] (V)--(A);
\draw[dashed] (V)--(B);
\fill (62:2.4) circle (2pt);
\fill (V) circle (2pt);
\pic[draw,"$\theta$",angle radius=1.1cm,angle eccentricity=1.35]{angle=A--V--B};
\node[below,font=\small] at (0,-0.25) {vertex};
\node[below,font=\small] at (2.0,-0.25) {base arm};
\node[font=\small,anchor=west] at (62:3.5) {rule the second arm};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
